@meta
id: 07-16
title: Jump to the bracket that closes it
chapter: 07
type: lesson
skills: match-percent, goto-line
par: 30
minutes: 5
vscode: click the opening brace, read the highlight, scroll down, click again
open: settlement.provider.ts

@brief
Three calls in this provider are not terminated. Their opening lines are 19,
40 and 72, and each call runs for another ten or fifteen lines before it
closes. Put a semicolon after the closing bracket of each.

Do not count lines to find the closing bracket. Do not scroll looking for
the indentation to line up. Vim has already matched the brackets in this
file and will take you straight there.

@keys
| `%` | jump to the matching bracket |
| `19G` | line 19 |
| `A` | append at the end of the line |

@hints
Brackets come in pairs and vim knows which pairs with which. One key asks it
to take you to the other end.
--
`%` jumps between a bracket and its match. It works on `(`, `[` and `{`, in
both directions. You do not even have to be sitting on the bracket: from
anywhere on the line, `%` takes the first bracket at or after the cursor
and jumps to its partner.
--
`19G` `%` and you are on the closing bracket, however many lines down it is.
Then `A` `;` `Esc` and on to the next one.

@solution
    19G % A ; <Esc>
    40G % A ; <Esc>
    72G % A ; <Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

Twenty-five keystrokes, and three of them crossed a total of forty lines.

`%` is the first motion in the dojo that understands the structure of the
text rather than its shape. `}` knows about blank lines, `w` knows about
punctuation, `f` knows about a character you named. `%` knows that this
`(` on line 19 belongs to that `)` on line 30, that the eight brackets in
between are balanced, and that none of them is the answer.

That last part is what makes it trustworthy on real code. The call on line
40 has nested objects three deep inside it, and `%` walks past all of them
without you having to think about it. Counting braces by eye is exactly the
kind of work you should never be doing, and it is the kind that goes wrong
quietly.

Three things worth knowing now. `%` works on `[` and `]` and on `{` and `}`
as well as parens. It works backwards, so from a closing bracket it takes
you to the opening one and pressing it twice returns you to where you
started. And it is a motion like any other, so from chapter 8 it takes an
operator, and `d%` deletes an entire balanced block in two keystrokes.

@start settlement.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { SettlementRepository } from './settlement.repository';
import { OrderRepository } from '../order/order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';
import { ReportService } from '../../infrastructure/services/report.service';
import { SettleBatchArgs, SettlementReportArgs } from './settlement.types';

@injectable()
export class SettlementProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(SettlementRepository) protected settlementRepository: SettlementRepository,
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
    @inject(ReportService) protected reportService: ReportService,
  ) {}

  public async settleBatch(args: SettleBatchArgs) {
    const orders = await this.orderRepository.findMany({
      where: {
        status: 'COMPLETED',
        settledAt: null,
        placedAt: {
          gte: args.from,
          lte: args.to,
        },
      },
      orderBy: { placedAt: 'asc' },
      take: args.take ?? 500,
    })

    if (!orders.length) {
      throw exception('SETTLEMENT_BATCH_EMPTY');
    }

    return orders;
  }

  public async recordSettlement(orderId: string, reference: string) {
    const settlement = await this.settlementRepository.create({
      data: {
        orderId,
        reference,
        status: 'SETTLED',
        settledAt: new Date(),
        provider: {
          name: 'acme-payments',
          environment: process.env.PAYMENTS_ENV ?? 'production',
        },
        amounts: {
          gross: 0,
          fees: 0,
          net: 0,
        },
      },
    })

    return settlement;
  }

  public async publishReport(args: SettlementReportArgs) {
    const rows = await this.settlementRepository.findMany({
      where: {
        settledAt: {
          gte: args.from,
          lte: args.to,
        },
      },
      orderBy: { settledAt: 'asc' },
    });

    return this.reportService.deliver({
      name: `settlement-${args.from.toISOString()}`,
      recipients: [
        'finance@acme.dev',
        'payments@acme.dev',
      ],
      format: 'csv',
      rows: rows.map((row) => ({
        order: row.orderId,
        reference: row.reference,
        amount: row.amounts.net,
        settled: row.settledAt,
      })),
    })
  }

  public async markFailed(orderId: string, reason: string) {
    return this.settlementRepository.update({
      where: { orderId },
      values: { status: 'FAILED', reason },
    });
  }

  public async summary(args: SettlementReportArgs) {
    const rows = await this.settlementRepository.findMany({
      where: { settledAt: { gte: args.from, lte: args.to } },
    });

    return {
      count: rows.length,
      net: rows.reduce((total, row) => total + row.amounts.net, 0),
    };
  }
}

